<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-docker-sandboxes"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="Tested with Docker Sandboxes 0.39.0 on macOS on September 7, 2026."
      toc={[
        {"connect", "Connect Docker Sandboxes"},
        {"limits-and-risks", "Limits & risks"},
        {"cleanup", "Remove the setup"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-docker-sandboxes">
        <:dek>
          Run a coding agent in a Docker microVM and give it emisar through Docker's MCP gateway.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Docker Sandboxes isolates the agent from <a
          href={~p"/docs/connect-docker-sandboxes#limits-and-risks"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >most of your host</a>. The emisar bridge stays on the host, where Docker's gateway can
        reach it without copying your emisar key into the VM.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Docker Sandboxes installed on a supported macOS or Ubuntu host, and a Docker account.
          Follow Docker's <.link
            href="https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >installation guide</.link>.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Access to Codex and a project directory containing only files the agent should be able
          to edit. Docker Sandboxes handles model authentication on the host when you first run it.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">quickstart</.link>
          sets up a runner and its first action.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_sandbox_connection
        name="Docker Sandboxes"
        connect_href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
        audit_href={~p"/app/audit"}
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="limits-and-risks">Limits &amp; risks</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Docker shares the project directory with the VM. That means the agent can see anything
          stored there and potentially leak it, including secrets in
          <.docs_inline_code>.env</.docs_inline_code>
          files and temporary artifacts. For stronger isolation from local files, try <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/connect-coop"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >co:op</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          The registered MCP launcher runs on the host with your permissions. Keep it and its
          environment file outside the shared project and never let the agent edit them.
        </li>
        <li>
          We recommend reviewing the sandbox's network policy and allowing access only to the
          services the agent needs.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="cleanup">Remove the setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Remove the sandbox and its MCP registration in Docker, then revoke the dedicated key from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        before deleting the owner-only launcher files.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
